novelty_contribution:
{
  "theoretical_novelty": "This research introduces a novel theoretical framework linking interpretable internal representations in LLMs to user trust and safety outcomes, positing that enhanced interpretability mediates user engagement and reduces anxiety in multilingual contexts. This framework advances understanding of how interpretability influences user behavior, particularly in non-English interactions where safety risks are pronounced.",
  "methodological_novelty": "Employing a three-arm design with distinct intervention and control groups allows for rigorous comparative analysis of the effects of interpretable representations on user trust and safety. The use of custom interpretability metrics and a safety response framework provides a robust methodology for evaluating the effectiveness of the proposed interventions, addressing gaps in empirical validation of interpretability in LLMs.",
  "practical_translational_novelty": "By focusing on multilingual applications, this study aims to develop practical strategies that enhance user understanding and trust in LLMs, thereby improving safety in diverse linguistic contexts. The findings will contribute to the establishment of universal safety standards for LLM deployment, making the research relevant for real-world applications in global AI interactions."
}

